476
00:30:49,648 --> 00:30:51,412
We'd offer you a drink. But

477
00:30:51,583 --> 00:30:53,245
- we're all out.
- Ha-ha-ha!

478
00:30:53,418 --> 00:30:57,082
Uh, I'll take care of that.

479
00:30:58,190 --> 00:31:00,659
So, um, heh, what
are we celebrating?

480
00:31:00,826 --> 00:31:03,796
Oh, Carrie's just decided what
she's gonna be when she grows up.

481
00:31:03,962 --> 00:31:07,455
- Oh, yeah? And that is?
- A sex therapist. Ha, ha.

482
00:31:08,200 --> 00:31:10,692
Waitress... Um...

483
00:31:10,869 --> 00:31:13,304
May we have one more pitcher
and an extra glass please?

484
00:31:13,472 --> 00:31:14,712
I'll be glad to serve you, sir,

485
00:31:14,840 --> 00:31:17,320
but I'm afraid the bartender
thinks the ladies have had enough.

486
00:31:17,476 --> 00:31:19,911
What? What kind of deal is that?

487
00:31:20,078 --> 00:31:21,205
Actually, it's the law.

488
00:31:21,380 --> 00:31:24,220
If you got in an accident on the way
home, he could be held responsible.

489
00:31:27,285 --> 00:31:32,986
I know that, uh, because, um,
because I get cut off all the time, heh.

490
00:31:33,158 --> 00:31:35,638
Come on, Carrie, I think we
should take our business elsewhere.

491
00:31:35,794 --> 00:31:37,956
- Whoa! Whoa! Whoa!
- Ha-ha-ha.

492
00:31:38,130 --> 00:31:40,531
I think that you'd
better call it a night.

493
00:31:40,699 --> 00:31:42,998
I think we'd better call a cab, heh.
- Heh.

494
00:31:43,168 --> 00:31:47,435
I have an idea. Why
don't you I drive you home?

495
00:31:50,375 --> 00:31:52,503
Oh, well, heh.

496
00:31:54,913 --> 00:31:57,678
Hey. This is a nice
place. Are you renting it?

497
00:31:57,849 --> 00:31:59,511
Whoa, oh, I'm sorry.

498
00:31:59,684 --> 00:32:01,744
No. It's my aunt's. She's
in Europe for the summer.

499
00:32:01,920 --> 00:32:06,858
Oh. Does, uh, she have
any idea how messy you are.

500
00:32:07,025 --> 00:32:10,962
- Oh, it's all Ashley's stuff.
- Very funny.

501
00:32:11,129 --> 00:32:13,860
- Hey, would you get me a beer.
- Oh, me too.

502
00:32:14,900 --> 00:32:16,630
Oh, I think you two
have had enough.

503
00:32:16,802 --> 00:32:19,135
I'll make you some coffee.

504
00:32:19,671 --> 00:32:20,730
My name's Cliff.

505
00:32:20,906 --> 00:32:23,398
- Heh. Ha-ha-ha.
- It's Bozo.

506
00:32:23,575 --> 00:32:25,976
I've never seen anyone drive
a Ferrari so slow in my life.

507
00:32:26,144 --> 00:32:28,613
- Ha-ha-ha.
- What do you want in your coffee?

508
00:32:28,780 --> 00:32:30,340
Cream and sugar. - Kahlua.

509
00:32:34,486 --> 00:32:35,852
Hey, you know what?

510
00:32:36,021 --> 00:32:37,751
Your sugar is as hard as a rock.

511
00:32:37,923 --> 00:32:40,620
Oh, humidity gets to it
after a while. It's still good.

512
00:32:56,074 --> 00:32:59,135
I gotta go. I just remembered.
There's some place that I gotta be.

513
00:32:59,311 --> 00:33:01,246
- I'm really sorry.
- It's almost midnight.

514
00:33:01,413 --> 00:33:03,853
Yeah. Oh, yeah, that's... Whoa,
wow. That's why I gotta hurry.

515
00:33:03,982 --> 00:33:05,782
Um, I'll catch you on
the beach at some point.

516
00:33:05,917 --> 00:33:07,517
There's a volleyball
tournament tomorrow.

517
00:33:07,686 --> 00:33:09,712
Yeah. At 1:00. Heh,
you wanna play?

518
00:33:09,888 --> 00:33:14,952
No, uh, no. I'll just watch.
I'll watch. Thanks. See you.

519
00:33:15,994 --> 00:33:17,826
Bozo.

520
00:33:29,741 --> 00:33:31,972
Hello? CLIFF <i>Ben, it's Cliff.</i>

521
00:33:32,143 --> 00:33:34,942
I know it's late. But I just
discovered something.

522
00:33:35,113 --> 00:33:38,777
There is sugar in
Carrie Locke's house.

523
00:33:42,420 --> 00:33:43,581
<i>Ben?</i>

524
00:33:44,322 --> 00:33:45,756
<i>Ben, do you hear me?</i>

525
00:33:47,459 --> 00:33:49,826
Ben, wake up. MATLOCK: What?

526
00:33:49,995 --> 00:33:53,432
There is sugar in Carrie's
house, almost a full 5-pound bag.

527
00:33:53,598 --> 00:33:55,567
It looks like it's
been there for weeks.

528
00:34:01,473 --> 00:34:02,998
Sugar.

529
00:34:03,708 --> 00:34:05,176
Did you say sugar?

530
00:34:05,343 --> 00:34:09,371
Yes, Ben, I said sugar,
enough to bake 12 marble cakes.

531
00:34:43,248 --> 00:34:45,012
I don't know how they do it.

532
00:34:45,183 --> 00:34:47,709
If they were as drunk
last night as Cliff said.

533
00:34:47,886 --> 00:34:49,787
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

534
00:34:49,955 --> 00:34:51,423
Whoo!

535
00:34:52,691 --> 00:34:56,560
- I wonder why that one... Carrie?
- Yeah.

536
00:34:56,728 --> 00:34:58,993
I wonder why she's
wearing that T-shirt.

537
00:34:59,164 --> 00:35:04,000
You'd think she'd
want to show off her tan.

538
00:35:05,003 --> 00:35:06,665
They make a nice
team, don't they?

539
00:35:06,838 --> 00:35:09,069
Volleyball, extortion, murder.

540
00:35:12,644 --> 00:35:14,636
- Of course, I know, we need the proof.
- Yeah.

541
00:35:14,813 --> 00:35:18,978
But the sugar that Cliff found proves
that Ashley didn't need to borrow

542
00:35:19,150 --> 00:35:20,390
any that day from her neighbor.

543
00:35:20,552 --> 00:35:23,545
She was just setting
up, uh, an alibi for Carrie.

544
00:35:24,556 --> 00:35:26,957
We could suggest that.

545
00:35:27,792 --> 00:35:29,124
We?

546
00:35:29,294 --> 00:35:32,696
Did you say we? I
heard you say we, Dad.

547
00:35:32,864 --> 00:35:35,129
Does that mean you wanna
be my co-counsel at the trial?

548
00:35:35,300 --> 00:35:36,893
No more "I'm on vacation"?

549
00:35:37,068 --> 00:35:39,230
Oh, Dad!

550
00:35:39,804 --> 00:35:41,295
Oh, Dad.

551
00:35:55,353 --> 00:35:56,616
Yes, yes, yes.

552
00:35:56,788 --> 00:35:59,223
All right, two more points.

553
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:17,871
You sure you don't
wanna take this?

554
00:36:18,043 --> 00:36:21,741
Oh, why don't you take it? Maybe
afterwards we can go on vacation.

555
00:36:21,913 --> 00:36:23,313
Hmm.

556
00:36:27,452 --> 00:36:29,978
The address you just gave

557
00:36:30,522 --> 00:36:34,926
is, uh, actually your
aunt's address, isn't it?

558
00:36:35,093 --> 00:36:38,029
That's right. She's in Europe
and we're house sitting.

559
00:36:38,797 --> 00:36:40,789
- We?
- Ashley Armstrong and I.

560
00:36:40,965 --> 00:36:43,594
She's a friend of mine from school.
- Nursing school.

561
00:36:43,768 --> 00:36:44,929
Yes.

562
00:36:45,103 --> 00:36:49,040
Are you, uh, are you working
here or just, uh, playing?

563
00:36:49,207 --> 00:36:50,436
No. Just playing.

564
00:36:52,777 --> 00:36:57,112
These are records from
the place where you bank

565
00:36:57,282 --> 00:36:58,978
here in Wilmington, Ms. Locke.

566
00:36:59,150 --> 00:37:03,884
And, uh, during June and July,
how many deposits did you make?

567
00:37:04,055 --> 00:37:06,718
- Three.
- How much money?

568
00:37:07,025 --> 00:37:10,257
- Five thousand dollars.
- Five thousand dollars.

569
00:37:10,428 --> 00:37:14,593
You just said you're not working.
Where did you get that kind of money?

570
00:37:14,766 --> 00:37:17,201
- It was a gift.
- Oh.

571
00:37:17,669 --> 00:37:19,831
- You don't wanna say, uh...?
- No. It's personal.

572
00:37:20,004 --> 00:37:22,030
- I'd rather not say.
- Oh, okay, okay.

573
00:37:22,207 --> 00:37:28,443
But, uh, but was it from, uh, was it
from, uh, Jerry Jennings back there?

574
00:37:28,613 --> 00:37:29,637
No.

575
00:37:29,814 --> 00:37:32,010
Well, you do know
Mr. Jennings, don't you?

576
00:37:32,550 --> 00:37:34,109
I went out with him once.

577
00:37:34,285 --> 00:37:35,981
What happened when
you went out with him?

578
00:37:36,154 --> 00:37:38,885
Or that is, when you
took him to your house?

579
00:37:40,325 --> 00:37:42,988
Well, he's prepared to testify

580
00:37:43,161 --> 00:37:46,825
that Tim McDaniels
showed up at your place

581
00:37:46,998 --> 00:37:49,160
while Jerry was there,

582
00:37:49,567 --> 00:37:53,561
claiming that you and your
roommate were trying to set Jerry up

583
00:37:53,738 --> 00:37:56,264
the same way you
had set him up. Tim.

584
00:37:56,808 --> 00:37:58,299
Wonder what that means.

585
00:37:58,476 --> 00:37:59,603
I have no idea.

586
00:37:59,778 --> 00:38:03,647
Oh, don't be modest. As
cons go, it was brilliant.

587
00:38:03,815 --> 00:38:08,185
You were gonna
let him go just so far.

588
00:38:08,553 --> 00:38:10,715
And then you were
gonna cry rape.

589
00:38:11,656 --> 00:38:13,090
You know. Date rape.

590
00:38:13,258 --> 00:38:15,853
Forcing Jerry to decide
whether to pay you off

591
00:38:16,027 --> 00:38:19,395
or face the humiliation
of being arrested.

592
00:38:19,564 --> 00:38:20,759
No. LAWYER: Objection.

593
00:38:20,932 --> 00:38:22,924
- Excuse me. MATLOCK:
Trying to extort money

594
00:38:23,101 --> 00:38:25,536
from Jerry the same way
you extorted money from Tim.

595
00:38:25,703 --> 00:38:26,762
- No.
- I object!

596
00:38:26,938 --> 00:38:29,058
That's where the money in
your bank account came from.

597
00:38:29,207 --> 00:38:30,470
- Isn't it?
- No.

598
00:38:30,642 --> 00:38:31,974
- Objection.
- Mr. Matlock.

599
00:38:34,345 --> 00:38:35,904
Yes, sir?

600
00:38:36,648 --> 00:38:38,617
- Knock it off.
- Knock what off?

601
00:38:38,783 --> 00:38:43,812
Making unfounded accusations, trying
to pass wild speculation off as fact.

602
00:38:43,988 --> 00:38:45,508
- Your Honor...
- If I have to use

603
00:38:45,657 --> 00:38:48,889
my gavel again, Mr. Matlock,
you're going to regret it.

604
00:38:49,060 --> 00:38:51,552
- Is that clear?
- Yes, sir.

605
00:38:53,164 --> 00:38:55,656
Now, regardless of the veracity,

606
00:38:55,834 --> 00:39:00,135
the truth of Mr. McDaniels'
allegations that night,

607
00:39:00,305 --> 00:39:06,245
he did indicate that he was going to
hassle you for the rest of the summer.

608
00:39:06,411 --> 00:39:08,141
- Didn't he?
- Yes.

609
00:39:08,313 --> 00:39:10,475
- You didn't like that?
- Heh, of course not.

610
00:39:10,648 --> 00:39:12,207
Would you please tell the court

611
00:39:12,383 --> 00:39:14,614
what happened to
Mr. McDaniels the very next day?

612
00:39:15,386 --> 00:39:16,445
He drowned.

613
00:39:17,055 --> 00:39:21,584
And who was the lifeguard who
swam out and tried to save him?

614
00:39:21,759 --> 00:39:23,057
Mike Rydell.

615
00:39:23,228 --> 00:39:24,856
- The decedent.
- Yes.

616
00:39:25,029 --> 00:39:26,589
A very interesting coincidence.

617
00:39:27,465 --> 00:39:31,232
- Did you know Mike Rydell?
- Yes.

618
00:39:31,402 --> 00:39:33,803
So well, that you asked him

619
00:39:33,972 --> 00:39:36,134
to get rid of Tim McDaniels
for you, didn't you?

620
00:39:36,307 --> 00:39:37,366
No. LAWYER: Objection.

621
00:39:37,542 --> 00:39:40,376
So he did. But then
he became the problem

622
00:39:40,545 --> 00:39:42,185
and you had to get
rid of him, didn't you?

623
00:39:42,313 --> 00:39:43,406
- No.
- Objection.

624
00:39:43,581 --> 00:39:47,177
When you saw him slug my client
on the beach the very next day,

625
00:39:47,352 --> 00:39:51,016
knowing he was diabetic, you
saw a way to go through with it?

626
00:39:51,189 --> 00:39:52,657
- It's not true.
- Your Honor.

627
00:39:52,824 --> 00:39:56,022
So you went to Clyde Eller's
house, stole one of his used syringes,

628
00:39:56,194 --> 00:39:58,163
used a rag or
gloves or something

629
00:39:58,329 --> 00:40:00,569
- to keep your fingerprints off of it.
- That's not true.

630
00:40:00,732 --> 00:40:02,633
Filled it with
Monocaine, - Excuse me.

631
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:05,240
Went back to Mike Rydell's
place and injected him. Didn't you?

632
00:40:05,303 --> 00:40:06,896
- No.
- Excuse me.

633
00:40:07,071 --> 00:40:10,007
And then, after he died, you
picked up the phone again,

634
00:40:10,174 --> 00:40:12,370
didn't leave fingerprints,
and called Ashley,

635
00:40:12,543 --> 00:40:16,207
so she could implicate Clyde
Eller when the police questioned her.

636
00:40:17,382 --> 00:40:18,441
Don't!

637
00:40:23,588 --> 00:40:26,854
- You wanna say that again?
- Uh.

638
00:40:27,659 --> 00:40:31,460
Please, Your Honor, don't.

639
00:40:32,330 --> 00:40:33,559
I have proof.

640
00:40:34,666 --> 00:40:36,100
- You have proof?
- Yes, sir.

641
00:40:36,267 --> 00:40:42,434
Proof that Ms. Locke was in the
decedent's apartment that day.

642
00:40:42,607 --> 00:40:45,008
- Let's see it, Mr. Matlock.
- Right here, right here.

643
00:40:45,176 --> 00:40:47,042
Here, Your Honor. Right here.

644
00:40:47,679 --> 00:40:52,640
Now, uh, you play volleyball.

645
00:40:52,817 --> 00:40:54,581
Tell the court what this is.

646
00:40:54,752 --> 00:40:57,513
It's a trophy they gave out at the
volleyball championships last year.

647
00:40:57,655 --> 00:40:59,988
I borrowed this from,
uh, one of the winners.

648
00:41:00,591 --> 00:41:02,958
It's, uh, it's pretty neat.

649
00:41:03,127 --> 00:41:06,894
It's a clock and a
trophy all rolled into one.

650
00:41:07,065 --> 00:41:09,967
See that? It's nice, heh.

651
00:41:10,134 --> 00:41:13,571
- Did Mike Rydell have one of these?
- How should I know?

652
00:41:13,738 --> 00:41:16,765
He won a championship
match with Kip Vogel last year.

653
00:41:16,941 --> 00:41:19,069
So, yes, he had one.

654
00:41:19,410 --> 00:41:21,106
The thing is,
nobody could find it.

655
00:41:21,279 --> 00:41:23,544
Not in his apartment.
Not in his car.

656
00:41:23,715 --> 00:41:26,947
But the weird thing is, the
lady who cleans his place

657
00:41:27,118 --> 00:41:31,647
said she saw one exactly like
this on the nightstand by his bed

658
00:41:31,823 --> 00:41:37,922
that afternoon, just hours
before he was murdered.

659
00:41:38,663 --> 00:41:40,461
What happened to it, Carrie?

660
00:41:41,466 --> 00:41:42,695
How should I know?

661
00:41:42,867 --> 00:41:44,827
Mike struggled with you
before he died. Didn't he?

662
00:41:44,902 --> 00:41:46,200
I was nowhere near his place.

663
00:41:46,371 --> 00:41:48,091
- Excuse me. MATLOCK:
He grabbed that trophy

664
00:41:48,239 --> 00:41:49,901
and swung it at you. - No.

665
00:41:50,074 --> 00:41:51,736
- Excuse me. MATLOCK:
And he caught you

666
00:41:51,909 --> 00:41:54,743
on the arm, made two deep cuts
here, so deep they keep opening up,

667
00:41:54,912 --> 00:41:57,609
and they mark you, they
mark you as a murderer.

668
00:41:57,782 --> 00:41:59,011
Excuse me. CARRIE: No.

669
00:41:59,183 --> 00:42:01,448
- But...
- Excuse me.

670
00:42:01,619 --> 00:42:03,247
Mr. Matlock,

671
00:42:03,755 --> 00:42:06,657
can't you hear me
saying "Excuse me"?

672
00:42:10,061 --> 00:42:11,085
Yes, sir.

673
00:42:11,262 --> 00:42:13,731
That doesn't mean I
want to leave the room.

674
00:42:14,365 --> 00:42:17,358
- I know, sir.
- I gave you fair warning.

675
00:42:17,535 --> 00:42:21,131
Your Honor, make
her roll up her sleeve.

676
00:42:21,305 --> 00:42:23,171
Please. Her left sleeve.

677
00:42:23,341 --> 00:42:25,139
If there's nothing there,

678
00:42:25,309 --> 00:42:26,675
you can bang your gavel

679
00:42:26,844 --> 00:42:29,712
and I'll go straight to the pokey
without a word, cross my heart.

680
00:42:31,315 --> 00:42:35,377
I can't pass up a deal like that.
Push up your left sleeve, please.

681
00:42:39,657 --> 00:42:43,219
Push it up or I will hold
you in contempt, young lady.

682
00:42:45,530 --> 00:42:46,930
I wanna talk to a lawyer.

683
00:42:54,906 --> 00:42:56,841
You're a very
pretty girl, Carrie.

684
00:42:57,375 --> 00:42:58,968
So is Ashley.

685
00:42:59,644 --> 00:43:03,081
But somewhere back
there, you made a wrong turn.

686
00:43:04,248 --> 00:43:08,083
But I think we all know
what happened to Mike Rydell

687
00:43:08,252 --> 00:43:11,984
and how it happened and what
happened to Tim McDaniels.

688
00:43:12,490 --> 00:43:19,124
And I think we all know that
Clyde Eller had nothing to do with it.

689
00:43:21,299 --> 00:43:24,861
You're a very patient man,
Your Honor. Thank you.

690
00:43:46,157 --> 00:43:48,023
- Oh, I'll get it.
- Oh, good, good.

691
00:43:48,192 --> 00:43:49,216
Come on, Pop.

692
00:43:49,393 --> 00:43:51,153
Clyde helped me make
sandwiches for the road.

693
00:43:51,295 --> 00:43:52,888
Oh, please, it was
the least I could do.

694
00:43:53,064 --> 00:43:55,556
You're going running awfully
early this morning, aren't you?

695
00:43:55,733 --> 00:43:57,895
- Well.
- You can run but you can't hide.

696
00:43:58,069 --> 00:44:00,504
You haven't heard
the last from me, pal.

697
00:44:00,671 --> 00:44:02,503
I'm going straight
to my real lawyer.

698
00:44:02,673 --> 00:44:03,753
- You didn't tell him?
- No.

699
00:44:03,908 --> 00:44:06,503
- Tell me what?
- Clyde signed the house over to me

700
00:44:06,677 --> 00:44:09,670
yesterday in lieu of my fee.

701
00:44:09,981 --> 00:44:13,440
- You own this house?
- Well, yeah.

702
00:44:13,618 --> 00:44:15,177
It's not fair.

703
00:44:15,353 --> 00:44:16,719
You left her, broke her heart,

704
00:44:16,888 --> 00:44:19,858
I had to take care of her
year after year after year

705
00:44:20,024 --> 00:44:22,584
and she left me, took
half of everything I had

706
00:44:22,760 --> 00:44:25,594
and you, one of the richest
men in the world and here's me,

707
00:44:25,763 --> 00:44:28,426
a penniless jackass and
you end up with her house.

708
00:44:28,599 --> 00:44:31,569
I signed it over to
you this morning.

709
00:44:40,011 --> 00:44:41,035
Ooh.

710
00:44:51,889 --> 00:44:54,882
- I see you told him about the house.
- Yeah.

711
00:44:59,130 --> 00:45:01,099
Congratulations, Pop.

712
00:45:01,632 --> 00:45:04,466
Did you, uh, tell him
about the mortgage?

713
00:45:04,635 --> 00:45:07,002
- No.
- What mortgage?

714
00:45:07,171 --> 00:45:11,575
Well, the $4000 mortgage
payments you'll have to make.

715
00:45:11,742 --> 00:45:13,540
- Four thousand?
- Every month.

716
00:45:13,711 --> 00:45:15,431
How about the
problem with the roof?

717
00:45:15,580 --> 00:45:19,483
- What problem with the roof?
- It leaks. All over.

718
00:45:19,650 --> 00:45:21,881
It has to be replaced.

719
00:45:22,053 --> 00:45:23,749
I'd do the wiring first, though.

720
00:45:23,921 --> 00:45:29,383
- What's wrong with the wiring?
- It was done on the cheap.

721
00:45:30,661 --> 00:45:34,291
The whole place
could go up in flames.

722
00:45:34,966 --> 00:45:38,459
But now that it's all over
and you have the house,

723
00:45:38,636 --> 00:45:42,903
I'm sure that's the way
Lucy would have wanted it.

724
00:45:43,407 --> 00:45:47,174
And remember our...
Our horseshoe game?

725
00:45:47,345 --> 00:45:51,715
Mine was a leaner. You won.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
